---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="parallel-agents">

          <p>
            Run separate lines of work as separate agents, each in its own Git worktree or a managed copy of the repository, so
            unfinished work does not alter the main files. Watch them from a single dashboard and merge only what you
            trust.
          </p>

          <h2 id="agent-workspaces">Agent workspaces</h2>
          <p>
            Use another workspace when work can happen independently: one agent can handle the
            interface while another investigates tests or infrastructure. Vector sets no cap on how many of its own
            agents run at once; coding agents you installed yourself run up to four at once.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><span>1</span><div><strong>Create a workspace</strong><p>Name the responsibility and send its first request.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>2</span><div><strong>Keep working</strong><p>Switch between workspaces while they stream activity independently.</p></div></li>
            <li><span>3</span><div><strong>Review and merge</strong><p>Inspect the diff and checks before bringing selected work into main.</p></div></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="note">
            An isolated agent gets a Git worktree on its own branch only when the folder is a Git repository and the
            working tree is clean. Without one — no repository, uncommitted changes, or a worktree that cannot be
            created — Vector provisions an isolated copy instead and records which it used in the workspace log.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            Two agents should not intentionally edit the same lines at the same time. Split work by
            responsibility to reduce conflicts and make review easier.
          </p>

          <h2 id="isolated-subagents">Isolated subagents and agent workspaces</h2>
          <p>
            {release113.since} a subagent the main agent launches with <code>isolation: "worktree"</code> also works in
            a git worktree of its own, but it is not an agent workspace. It starts inside your session, reports to the
            main agent, and its changes come back to Background tasks as "N files changed · branch" for you or the agent
            to review, merge or discard; when it changes nothing, its worktree is removed. An agent workspace or a Swarm
            worker is a session you start, watch in the Agent Dashboard and merge yourself. See
            <a href="/docs/subagents#worktrees">Helpers in their own worktree</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} the subagents that an agent workspace or a Swarm worker starts share the limits of the
            conversation it was started from: 8 running at once, 32 per request and one $25 spend ceiling, so a fan-out
            across workspaces does not multiply them. Stop including helpers in that conversation leaves agent
            workspaces and Swarm workers running; stop them from the Agent Dashboard.
          </p>

          <h2 id="change-branch">Change an agent's branch</h2>
          <p>
            The session header shows the branch the agent's checkout is on. Click it to switch that checkout to
            another local branch, or type a new name and choose Create branch to make one from the current commit.
            Vector runs an ordinary <code>git switch</code>: it never stashes or discards your work, so a switch that
            would overwrite uncommitted changes is refused with git's reason. Branches already checked out in another
            agent's workspace are shown but cannot be picked, and Vector refuses to switch while one of its agents is
            running in that checkout. In an agent workspace Vector manages, you can create a new branch but not switch to
            an existing one, because that workspace merges back from the commit it started on.
          </p>

          <h2 id="coordinated-runs">Coordinated runs: one objective, a team of agents</h2>
          <p>
            Instead of naming each workspace yourself, choose <strong>Coordinate agents</strong> in the launcher and
            give Vector the objective. A planner model reads the repository and writes a plan: tasks with roles —
            explore, implement, debug, test, review, security, docs — and the dependencies between them. Each task
            becomes its own agent in its own workspace, routed to a model by tier, and tasks run as soon as the ones
            they depend on finish. Explore, debug, test, review and security tasks go to the matching subagent
            specialist; implement and docs tasks run on the general agent.
          </p>
          <p>
            You set the strategy (economy, balanced or quality), the most agents that may run at once, and how many
            run in parallel. The run reports as one board — planning, running, needs review — and nothing reaches your
            main checkout until you look at the combined diff and merge it.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Teams can be wired everyone-to-everyone, coordinator-to-workers, or any pairing you set. A message to a
            teammate you have not linked is refused and told so, rather than dropped where the sender never finds out.
          </p>

          <h2 id="agent-dashboard">The Agent Dashboard</h2>
          <p>
            Every agent in the workspace on one board: <strong>Running</strong>, <strong>Needs you</strong> and
            <strong>Done</strong>. Open it from the workspace rail. Switch to a list for a denser view of the same
            three groups.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Needs you</strong><span>Merges failed with needs-review, including a finished run that reported an error. Both are stopped and waiting on a person.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Filters that compose</strong><span>Status, Team, Pull request and Agent. Two values in one menu means either; values in two menus means both.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Pull request state</strong><span>Open, merged, or never opened — merge state wins, so a merged run does not sit under "open" forever.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Teams as a unit</strong><span>Agents sharing a team or swarm filter together, and the board shows the messages they sent each other mid-run.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            Two agents holding uncommitted work on the same file are reported as a clash. Merging both will conflict —
            review one before merging the other.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Agent workspaces are not subagents. Subagents work inside one session's checkout and report to its main
            agent; see <a href="/docs/subagents">Subagents and subagent specialists</a>.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
